<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ilorin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ilorin is a city in west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> that was originally a provincial centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>. Its revolt in the early nineteenth century, followed by its incorporation into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, contributed to the collapse of Oyo. It became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> serving as a southern base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ilorin.jpg" alt="Ilorin" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eukoha · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilorin_palace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ilorin lies where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>'s northern savanna meets the southwestern forest belt, in the transitional Middle Belt, about 300 kilometres north-northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>. The Asa is the longest of the rivers that drain the area. Its people speak mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, in a local dialect known as Ilorin Yoruba, while the ruling dynasty is of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> origin and its titles, festivals and Islamic scholarship tie it closely to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>–Fulani north. Scholars therefore describe it as a meeting point of north and south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Oral tradition holds that the area was settled for many centuries by successive groups. Tradition credits Ojo Isekuse, a hunter and blacksmith, as one of the early founders, and traces the city's name to okuta ilọ irin, the stone for sharpening iron. By the 18th century Ilorin was a border settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> with a mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> population and growing numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> immigrants.</p><p>In the early 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonja">Afonja</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> field marshal (Aare Ona Kakanfo) stationed at Ilorin, rebelled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaafin">Alaafin</a> with the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shehu-alimi">Shehu Alimi</a>. Their alliance collapsed in a struggle for power and Afonja was killed. Alimi's eldest son Abd al-Salam became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Ilorin, receiving a flag from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> and bringing Ilorin into the Sokoto Caliphate as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>, traditionally dated between 1823 and 1831.</p><p>Ilorin repelled an Oyo-led coalition at the Battle of Ilorin, around 1835, an event that hastened the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-ile">Old Oyo</a>. The army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadan">Ibadan</a> halted its southward expansion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a> in 1840. Through the century it was a major trading node between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> south and a centre of Islamic learning.</p><p>Ilorin resisted British rule but was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-niger-company">Royal Niger Company</a> in 1897 and incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-nigeria-protectorate">Northern Nigeria Protectorate</a> in 1900. In 1967 it became the capital of the newly created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwara-state">Kwara State</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ilorin is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwara-state">Kwara State</a> and the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilorin-emirate">Ilorin Emirate</a>, ruled by eleven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, all from the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shehu-alimi">Shehu Alimi</a>. Islam is the majority religion, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> is widely practised. The Ilorin Central Mosque, begun in 1977 and opened in 1981, has 99 domes and four minarets. Its Durbar, a procession of decorated horses and riders led by the Emir, is held a day or two after Eid al-Adha. The city has some of the largest traditional pottery workshops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and produces hand-woven aso-oke textiles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ilorin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1817 Afonja, based at <b>Ilorin</b>, rebelled openly and enlisted the support of the Fulani Muslim cleric Alimi and Hausa and Fulani followers inspired by the Sokoto jihad, which brought a dangerous new enemy into the centre of the empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Later operations extended the caliphate southward into Yoruba territory: <b>Ilorin</b>, where the Oyo general Afonja rebelled around 1817 with Fulani and Muslim support, passed under a Fulani emir in the 1820s and, as the Ilorin Emirate, became the caliphate's spearhead against the Oyo Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ilorin</b> was a frontier town of the Oyo Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/ilorin-emirate">Ilorin Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
